  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe work week will begin under zonal flow aloft as the high \namplitude, positively-tilted ridge that was responsible for the \nanomalous warmth over the weekend progresses towards the east. A \ncold front is expected to push across SE Wyoming and Western \nNebraska sometime Monday morning, which will introduce a cooler air \nmass relative to the near record-breaking temperatures that are \nexpected on Sunday. With PWAT largely below the 50th percentile per \nlong range ensemble guidance and any large-scale forcing for ascent, \nprecipitation chances look minimal at this time. The main noticeable \nchange will be the 10-15 degree drop in high temperatures, with \nlocations west of the I-25 corridor seeing highs in the mid to upper \n80s, and our Nebraska counties barely peaking above 90 degrees. \nNeedless to say, these temperatures are still running at or even \nslightly above average. Gusty winds are also possible on Monday, as \ngradient flow increases thanks to lee cyclogenesis occurring to\nour south over Colorado combined with a surface high building \nin behind the cold front. With 50th percentile wind gusts \nranging from 25-30 mph, minimum RH values decreasing to 15-25% \nduring the afternoon, and plenty of dry fuels with the D3-D4 \ndrought conditions, fire weather concerns persist into early \nnext week despite the slightly cooler temperatures. \n\nOn Tuesday, a secondary shot of cooler air will extend down into our \narea as a surface anticyclone becomes established to our north over \nthe Dakotas. With weak isentropic descent/cold advection behind the \nfront, high temperatures are expected to be 10-15 degrees cooler \nthan Monday's highs, with many locations struggling to reach \nthe 80 degree mark. This is a much welcome change to what we \nhave seen so far this summer, but don't get too excited \nconsidering trends into next weekend, as I'm sure many of you \nare burning to know...\n\nExpect a slight increase in high temperatures on Wednesday as a 594 \ndam mid-level ridge builds across the Desert Southwest, with high \ntemperatures at or slightly below climatology (average high for \nCheyenne on Wednesday is 84 degrees). We still look to remain mostly \ndry as SE Wyoming/W Nebraska remain under WNW flow aloft, which \nisn't exactly favorable for monsoonal moisture transport from the \nsouthwest. The ridge (as does the heat) builds on Thursday, with \nensemble guidance suggesting median high temperatures in the low to \nmid-90s east of the Laramie Range, and upper 80s to the west. With \nPWAT progged around 15-25% relative to climatology, we look to \nremain mostly dry. The trend continues on Friday into the weekend \n(unfortunately), as 500 mb heights rise and the ridge remains well-\nestablished over Nevada and Arizona. Under quasi-zonal flow along \nthe northern periphery of the ridge, there aren't any strong signals \nfor any substantial monsoonal moisture moving into our area. Sorry \nfor sounding like a broken record, or for advertising the potential \nfor more heat-relaated records being broken, we look to remain hot \nand dry into later next week.",
